WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.314
In part one, the robot reached seven

2
00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:10.004
zero with one a move and three b moves.

3
00:00:10.096 --> 00:00:12.679
Now we pack the whole system

4
00:00:12.772 --> 00:00:14.340
into one product.

5
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.666
Stand the two moves side by side

6
00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:20.455
as the columns of a matrix A.

7
00:00:20.455 --> 00:00:23.524
Stack the amounts in a vector x

8
00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:25.476
and the target in b.

9
00:00:25.476 --> 00:00:29.010
The system reads: A times x equals b.

10
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.935
Before we compute

11
00:00:30.935 --> 00:00:34.261
what is the first entry of A times x

12
00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:36.187
when x is one, three?

13
00:00:36.187 --> 00:00:38.200
Pause and work it out.

14
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.150
The first way is by rows.

15
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.709
Take row one, one and two

16
00:00:46.709 --> 00:00:50.027
and pair it with x: one times one

17
00:00:50.027 --> 00:00:53.060
plus two times three, is seven.

18
00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.706
Row two: three times one

19
00:00:55.706 --> 00:00:58.645
minus one times three, is zero.

20
00:00:58.645 --> 00:01:02.777
Each entry is a dot product of a row with x.

21
00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:04.890
One row, one equation

22
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.553
one line in the row picture

23
00:01:07.553 --> 00:01:11.410
and x is the point where the lines cross.

24
00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.832
The second way is by columns.

25
00:01:14.832 --> 00:01:17.380
Back on the robot's grid

26
00:01:17.478 --> 00:01:20.908
A times x is x copies of column one

27
00:01:21.006 --> 00:01:23.750
plus y copies of column two.

28
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.393
One copy of one, three.

29
00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:29.583
Three copies of two, minus one

30
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:32.275
which is six, minus three.

31
00:01:32.275 --> 00:01:36.662
Put them tip to tail, and we land on seven

32
00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:37.260
zero.

33
00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.808
The same vector as by rows.

34
00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:43.309
Rows give the answer one number at a time

35
00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:46.810
columns show where the whole vector lands.

36
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.896
A warning for when you type

37
00:01:49.988 --> 00:01:51.566
this into Python.

38
00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:52.494
In numpy

39
00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:56.670
A star x is not the matrix times the vector.

40
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.114
The star multiplies entry by entry

41
00:02:00.205 --> 00:02:03.026
and returns a two by two array.

42
00:02:03.026 --> 00:02:06.758
Look at its columns: one a, and three b

43
00:02:06.758 --> 00:02:10.580
the two pieces side by side, never added.

44
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.065
For the product, use the at sign.

45
00:02:14.065 --> 00:02:17.960
A at x adds the pieces, and gives seven

46
00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:18.530
zero.

47
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.440
Now three equations in three unknowns, x

48
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.391
y and z.

49
00:02:24.496 --> 00:02:27.876
In the row picture each equation

50
00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.094
is a plane in space

51
00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:33.790
and three planes are hard to draw.

52
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.269
The column picture asks a

53
00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:38.399
simple question instead

54
00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:41.380
how much of each of three columns?

55
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.298
One of column one, two of column two

56
00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:49.252
and three of column three add up to six

57
00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:50.409
three, two.

58
00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:54.170
So x is one, y is two, and z is three.

59
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.299
A number called the determinant is seven

60
00:02:58.299 --> 00:02:59.221
not zero.

61
00:02:59.221 --> 00:03:01.894
Lecture sixteen explains it.

62
00:03:01.894 --> 00:03:05.028
For now, it says every right side

63
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.240
has exactly one answer.

64
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.176
Keep the matrix

65
00:03:09.176 --> 00:03:12.651
but change the right side to two, three

66
00:03:12.651 --> 00:03:16.465
zero, which is column one plus column three.

67
00:03:16.465 --> 00:03:18.329
What is the solution?

68
00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:21.380
Pause and answer without computing.

69
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.923
One of column one, none of column two

70
00:03:28.923 --> 00:03:31.114
one of column three.

71
00:03:31.114 --> 00:03:35.077
So x is one, y is zero, and z is one

72
00:03:35.077 --> 00:03:39.040
the column picture gives it for free.

73
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.623
Now give the robot a bad second move

74
00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.219
two east and six north.

75
00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:49.140
That is just twice move a.

76
00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.285
Every mix of the two moves stays

77
00:03:52.372 --> 00:03:55.080
on one line through one, three.

78
00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.439
Scale them by any amounts

79
00:03:57.439 --> 00:03:59.972
even negative ones, and add

80
00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:02.330
you never leave that line.

81
00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.823
The target seven, five is off the line

82
00:04:05.823 --> 00:04:08.080
so no amounts can reach it.

83
00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.059
In the row picture

84
00:04:10.145 --> 00:04:12.718
the two equations are parallel

85
00:04:12.803 --> 00:04:14.776
lines that never cross.

86
00:04:14.776 --> 00:04:17.949
The columns give only one direction

87
00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:20.950
so M has rank one: it is singular.

88
00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:24.703
If the target is on the line, say seven

89
00:04:24.703 --> 00:04:27.955
twenty one, the second equation is three

90
00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.890
times the first: the very same line.

91
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.553
Then seven, zero works.

92
00:04:33.553 --> 00:04:37.073
So does five, one, and one, three

93
00:04:37.073 --> 00:04:39.185
and minus one, four

94
00:04:39.185 --> 00:04:41.800
and infinitely many more.

95
00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.370
Zero, one, or infinitely many

96
00:04:45.478 --> 00:04:48.161
a linear system never has

97
00:04:48.269 --> 00:04:50.630
exactly two solutions.

98
00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.780
Does three equations in three unknowns

99
00:04:54.874 --> 00:04:57.034
always mean one answer?

100
00:04:57.034 --> 00:04:57.534
No.

101
00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:58.890
In this matrix

102
00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.206
column three is column one plus column two.

103
00:05:02.206 --> 00:05:04.919
All three columns lie in one plane

104
00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:06.426
so the rank is two

105
00:05:06.426 --> 00:05:09.440
and most right sides cannot be reached.

106
00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.413
The same product sits inside

107
00:05:12.498 --> 00:05:14.293
every neural network.

108
00:05:14.378 --> 00:05:16.856
A linear layer takes an input

109
00:05:16.942 --> 00:05:18.993
x and returns W times x

110
00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:21.300
where W holds the weights.

111
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.137
Read it by columns

112
00:05:23.137 --> 00:05:25.954
each input feature owns one column

113
00:05:25.954 --> 00:05:28.850
scaled by the value of that feature.

114
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.242
Three times the first column

115
00:05:31.242 --> 00:05:33.142
plus one times the second

116
00:05:33.142 --> 00:05:36.240
plus one times the third, gives five, zero.

117
00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.599
Last, a habit for working with AI.

118
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.541
Suppose an assistant says the three

119
00:05:42.623 --> 00:05:45.728
by three system is solved by two, one

120
00:05:45.728 --> 00:05:46.300
three.

121
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.006
Multiply back.

122
00:05:48.006 --> 00:05:51.493
A times the claim is six, six, one

123
00:05:51.493 --> 00:05:54.012
but b is six, three, two.

124
00:05:54.012 --> 00:05:57.499
The first equation happens to hold

125
00:05:57.499 --> 00:06:00.793
which makes the claim look right.

126
00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.730
The other two fail.

127
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.683
Checking one equation is not enough.

128
00:06:05.683 --> 00:06:06.840
Check them all.

129
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.434
To sum up.

130
00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:12.997
By rows, each entry of A x is a dot product

131
00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:14.823
one per equation.

132
00:06:14.823 --> 00:06:18.980
By columns, A x is a mix of the columns.

133
00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.221
And a system has zero, one

134
00:06:22.221 --> 00:06:26.610
or infinitely many solutions, never two.

135
00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.871
Now you try it both ways: the matrix two

136
00:06:30.871 --> 00:06:34.782
zero, one, four, times the vector three

137
00:06:34.782 --> 00:06:35.831
minus one.

138
00:06:35.831 --> 00:06:38.406
Next lecture: elimination

139
00:06:38.406 --> 00:06:41.840
a step by step method that finds x.
